## Related work: King and Worthington (2026) · [AI-generated]{title="Written by OpenAI Codex at David Reinstein's request. Not new empirical findings or agreed changes to the research plan."} note, October 23, 2026

@kingWorthington2026Peerless report strong AI review performance against paid rapidPeer experts. This substantially overlaps with our high-quality human comparison; our clearest distinctions concern structured research ratings, uncertainty, and proposed prospective and human–AI studies. See the [AI-generated comparison and research priorities](peerless_context.qmd#sec-peerless-context) and [discussion](discussion.qmd#sec-peerless).
:::

Peer review is under strain. Reviewers are hard to find, turnaround times are lengthening, and the system costs an estimated $1.5 billion per year in the United States alone [@aczel2021billion]. At the same time, generative AI lowers the cost of producing polished manuscripts; in at least some fields, editors report submission growth that exceeds reviewer capacity, and explicitly link this trend to LLM-assisted writing [@spitzer2026submission]. This combination creates demand for automated support in editorial and pre-submission workflows.

Commercial AI reviewer products such as Refine [@refineFAQ] and QED Science [@qedScience2026] already market automated referee-like feedback directly to authors, while disclaiming that they substitute for human peer review. Their internal architectures are not publicly documented. Other tools are open. IsItCredible, run by The Catalogue of Errors Ltd, sold automated "Reviewer 2" reports before retiring its hosted service, and has released that pipeline and its prompts as open-source code [@isitcredible; @isitcredibleReviewer2]. OpenAIReview [@openAIReview2026] is an open-source pipeline whose "progressive" method reads a paper in sequence while keeping a running summary of its definitions, equations, and key claims, at roughly $4 per paper. Meanwhile, publishers are formalizing policies that restrict reviewer use of general-purpose AI tools[^index-elsevier] while permitting controlled in-house applications for screening tasks [@elsevierAIPolicy2025; @leung2026policies]. Our study complements these efforts with a baseline: how closely the simplest possible setup—a single prompt to a frontier model, with no bespoke pipeline—matches structured expert evaluations, both in its ratings and in the issues it raises.

[^index-elsevier]: For example, Elsevier's policy states that "reviewers must not upload submitted manuscripts, or any information from them, into AI Tools" [@elsevierAIPolicy2025, Reviewers FAQ].

These developments make the evidentiary gap salient: funders, editors, and policymakers need to know when AI evaluation outputs are trustworthy enough to use, and when they are unstable, biased, or manipulable. Recent work documents three interlocking concerns. Reproducibility can be "jagged" across models and time [@thomas2026jagged], and subtle task reframings can induce systematic output shifts reminiscent of specification search [@AsherMalzahnPersanoPaschalMyersHall2026P_Hack]. Adversarial manipulation is not hypothetical: invisible prompt-injection text can inflate LLM review scores in simulated peer review [@choi2026invisible]. And even without manipulation, AI reviews tend to be less thematically diverse and less focused on interpretation and originality than human reviews [@rajakumar2026peerreview], while LLM scoring exhibits range restriction and halo effects that distort agreement metrics [@wang2025psychometric]. The most direct recent comparison is more favorable to AI: on 124 rapidPeer submissions, @kingWorthington2026Peerless find that AI reviews win every decided overall comparison against paid, carefully selected human reviews when an AI model judges, and 26 of 30 judgments by three former journal editors (see our [comparison with that study](peerless_context.qmd#sec-peerless-context)).

The central question we address is therefore: how reliably can frontier LLMs evaluate research, relative to expert peer review and under realistic levels of rater disagreement? We study this question in a setting designed to make "expert judgment" observable and multi-dimensional rather than implicit.

We use [The Unjournal's](https://www.unjournal.org/) structured human evaluations as a reference signal. We prompt GPT-5 Pro—a frontier reasoning model—with the same rubric and guidelines used by human evaluators, then compare its ratings with available expert evaluations. The main run covers 60 economics and social-science working papers, with 47 human-matched papers; critique overlap is a separate GPT-5.2 Pro analysis of 12 eligible pairings. We ask whether a frontier LLM evaluation can approximate expert judgment, where systematic differences arise, and whether the model reveals characteristic AI preferences over research. GPT-5 Pro shows positive rank agreement with the human panel, alongside compressed ratings and uneven coverage of expert concerns. A paired bootstrap places uncertainty around an approximate comparison with human inter-rater agreement[^index-bootstrap]; it does not establish equivalence to an additional expert. Appendix results describe five additional models spanning different capability and cost tiers, with sample availability varying by model. These findings motivate testing LLMs as supplementary raters in structured evaluation pipelines.

[^index-bootstrap]: We resample papers with replacement 2,000 times. In each draw we recompute both the model–human and the human–human correlation on the same resampled papers, and take their difference after a reliability adjustment. The spread of that difference across draws gives the interval. See [Results](results.qmd) and [Methods](methods.qmd).

Our approach is minimal by design: each model receives the same PDF and a fixed rubric in a single prompt, with no iteration, retrieval augmentation, chain-of-thought scaffolding, or multi-step agentic loop. This provides a baseline for testing more elaborate evaluation procedures. Structured measurement schemas [@AsirvathamMokskiShleifer2026GPTMeasurement], iterative quality checks [@Zhang2025], and prompt-sensitivity controls [@AsherMalzahnPersanoPaschalMyersHall2026P_Hack] could improve performance, but added complexity could also amplify shared errors or introduce new biases. Their incremental value requires comparison on held-out papers.

The Unjournal setting is particularly well suited for this comparison. It commissions paid expert evaluations using a structured rubric covering seven percentile criteria with 90% credible intervals plus journal-tier predictions, and publishes the resulting packages openly rather than making binary accept/reject decisions—which may increase reviewer effort through accountability and transparency. The resulting ratings and critiques still exhibit substantial inter-rater variation; accordingly, we treat human evaluations as a high-quality but noisy reference signal, not ground truth. The rich, multi-dimensional data allow us to compare the priorities and calibration of humans and AI models across criteria and domains, while eventual publication outcomes for journal-tier predictions provide an external validation opportunity[^index-1] enabling a human-vs-LLM horse race. Finally, The Unjournal’s pipeline of future evaluations allows for clean out-of-training-data predictions, serving as a live testing lab for prospective validation.

[^index-1]: These represent verifiable publication outcomes, not statements about the "true quality" of the paper.


